# Registry data and release artifacts are byte-exact by contract.
#
# Every release payload is hashed and its SHA-256 is published in the manifest,
# and `coverage-snapshot.json` is a committed measurement whose digest must be
# reproducible by anyone who clones this repository. Git for Windows ships with
# `core.autocrlf=true` in its system config, which rewrites LF to CRLF on
# checkout — that would change the byte length and the digest of these files on
# a fresh clone while leaving them untouched here, so a reader could not confirm
# a published digest against the file they received.
#
# Marking these paths as not-text disables all end-of-line conversion for them,
# in both directions, regardless of the cloner's `core.autocrlf` setting.
#
# Scope is deliberately this directory only. The wider repository already holds
# files that Git has converted to CRLF in working trees, and they are documents
# and workflows where the conversion is harmless; changing their treatment now
# would produce a large diff unrelated to registry integrity.
#
# The reader itself is end-of-line independent — `coverage.discover_registry_root`
# produces the same release payload from a CRLF root as from an LF root, verified
# in review — so this file protects the byte identity of the committed artifact,
# not the correctness of the release it describes.

*.jsonl -text
*.json  -text
